### 1.12.1 (2026-06-09)
- Changed — Campaigns now accept a much wider range of optimizations. Any authored mechanism — custom kernels, weight-layout transforms, compute-graph passes, pipeline or communication restructuring, attention/KV-layout changes — is eligible as long as it targets a profiled bottleneck and produces a measured end-to-end win, not just the three legacy kernel categories.
  - Category becomes a non-binding 9-value descriptor; gate routing keys on the slice attacked (decode-kernel vs inter-kernel). Grounded in 12 live B200/B300 sessions where library-restructuring wins were previously blocked.
- Improved — The enable flag on a shipped optimization is now named for what it does (e.g. `VLLM_<SCOPE>_<MECHANISM>`), so a resulting PR reads clearly instead of leaking an internal tracking handle like `VLLM_OP004`.
- Fixed — Campaigns no longer deadlock when re-mining for the next optimization after one ships.
- Fixed — Agent teammates now shut down cleanly when a stage ends, instead of lingering with an open terminal pane.
- Fixed — Per-track build environments no longer fail with `No module named 'cutlass'`.
- Fixed — A champion clearing a stale compile cache no longer stalls on an approval that never arrives in an unattended campaign (uses a fresh cache root instead of a blocked `rm -rf`).

### 1.12.0 (2026-06-04)
- Improved — Champions now choose their optimization approach from the actual profiling data for the component they were assigned, rather than committing to an approach from the written analysis before any data is examined. This produces fewer wasted pivots mid-campaign.
  - Motivated by a session where all three champions selected from the report alone; one claimed a fusion that the activation cubin already performed, burning two pivots.
- Changed — Two champions may now target the same component and differentiate by mechanism, instead of the later one being forced to yield.

### 1.11.1 (2026-06-04)
- Changed — A campaign now keeps an optimization that delivers a 0.25% end-to-end speedup (was 1.0%), so smaller but real wins are no longer discarded.
- Improved — Each benchmark now runs 10 measurement iterations (was 5) for steadier, less noisy timings.
- Improved — The design debate runs a single round by default — a second round only fires when a champion raises an unaddressed critique or new evidence — so rounds finish faster.

### 1.11.0 (2026-06-02)
- New — Bring your own vLLM fork. Point a session at any public GitHub vLLM fork (private forks via an access token) and AMMO builds it from source. You watch the build live in the terminal, and the session goes ACTIVE the moment the build finishes.
  - Fork URLs are restricted to `https://github.com/...`; access tokens are encrypted at rest. GPUs are held through the whole build so the session is ready immediately. Session cards show a BUILDING badge (with elapsed time) and a clickable FAILED badge on error.
- Improved — Resuming a fork session now takes ~1-3 min instead of ~15-20 min, because the compiler cache is preserved across pause/resume.
- New — Campaigns now make their own call at decision points when running unattended, instead of stalling and waiting for you.
  - An investigator agent weighs each option against the campaign goal from primary evidence and returns a recommendation.
- New — One-command draft PR from a session's verified wins (the `ammo-session-to-pr` workflow): locate the session, diff its shipped optimizations against the base, and open a draft vLLM PR with the campaign evidence.
- Improved — The dashboard fleet is far more stable under load — pods no longer restart under cross-pod traffic.
  - The event loop no longer blocks on synchronous K8s/S3/disk calls; cross-pod proxy fan-out is parallelized and capped at one hop.
- Fixed — Creating a fork session no longer silently drops the fork URL for browsers running a stale cached UI (asset cache-buster bumped).
- Fixed — Kernel-speedup hero numbers and the mining/debate panels now render for new sessions (they were sometimes blank after an internal metrics refactor).
- Fixed — A `main`-branch session no longer fails to start during the nightly vLLM wheel-publish window (it now pins the build-verified commit).

